The make_set() function collects all unique values into a list, instead of just counting them:
Email
| where link has "baddomain.com"
| summarize recipients = make_set(recipient) by subject
Let's apply this to browsing activity. A bunch of users we seen going to the malicious domain nothing-to-see-here.net. One of them downloaded a suspicious tool from that domain.
OutboundNetworkEvents
| where url has  "n_____________"
| summarize _______(url) by _____
What was the name of the tool?
I instead did a little something like this:
OutboundNetworkEvents
| where url has "nothing-to-see-here.net"
| where url has "exe"
Answer: advanced-ip-scanner.exe

Write a query that shows, for each username, the list of all IP addresses they successfully logged in from, and the count of unique IPs they logged in from. Find the ones that login from more than 5 IPs. How many accounts are logged into from more than 5 IPs?
We are given:
AuthenticationEvents
| where _____ == ______
| summarize 
    _______(src_ip),
    _______(src_ip)
by username
| where ______ > 5
This is what I came up with:
AuthenticationEvents
| where result == "Successful Login"
| summarize 
    total_ips = dcount(src_ip)
by username
| where total_ips > 5
Answer: 15

The top operator combines sorting and limiting into one step:
ProcessEvents
| summarize execution_count = count() by process_name
| top 10 by execution_count
This returns the 10 most executed processes.
What is the name of the fifth most executed process?
Answer: msedgewebview2.exe


Section 3: Data Transformation
The project operator selects which columns to display:
AuthenticationEvents
| take 100
| where result == "Failed Login"
| project timestamp, username, src_ip
You can also rename columns with project:
AuthenticationEvents
| take 100
| project EventTime = timestamp, User = username, SourceIP = src_ip
The extend operator adds new columns without removing existing ones:
AuthenticationEvents
| take 100
| extend hour_of_day = hourofday(timestamp)

Write a query to find authentication events during off-hours (before 6 AM or after 6 PM). How many authentication events occurred during off-hours?
We are given:
AuthenticationEvents
| extend hour = hourofday(timestamp)
| where __ < __ or __ >= __
| count
This is what I came up with:
AuthenticationEvents
| extend hour = hourofday(timestamp)
| where hour < 6 or hour >= 18
| count
(Using military time/24-hour time to get the hour numbers)
And the answer was 3.

The iff() function adds conditional logic:
Syntax: iff(condition, value_if_true, value_if_false)
AuthenticationEvents
| extend status = iff(result == "Successful Login", "OK", "FAILED")
For multiple conditions, use case():
| extend risk = case(
    attempts > 100, "CRITICAL",
    attempts > 50, "HIGH",
    attempts > 10, "MEDIUM",
    "LOW"
)
The last value is the default when no conditions match.


Section 4: Advanced Filtering
The startswith operator finds values that begin with a pattern:
AuthenticationEvents
| where src_ip startswith "10.10.1.4"
How many events originate from IPs starting with 10.10.1.4?
Answer: 633

The next page shows us the endswith operator, which you can use your context-clues to figure out what that does.
How many emails were sent with links ending with .docx?
I came up with:
Email
| where link endswith ".docx"
Answer: 273

The in operator checks if a value matches any item in a list:
AuthenticationEvents
| where src_ip in ("10.10.16.14", "10.10.16.7", "10.10.16.24")
Use !in to exclude values.
Using the query below as a template, how many unique sender domains were observed that were not one of the following?
jojoshospital.org
kentuckypharmasupply.com
Template:
Email
| extend  sender_domain = tostring(split(sender, "@")[-1])
| where sender_domain
I wrote out:
Email
| extend sender_domain = tostring(split(sender, "@")[-1])
| where sender_domain !in ("jojoshospital.org", "kentuckypharmasupply.com")
| distinct sender_domain
(I couldn't get the syntax right for dcount, but I remembered the distinct operator from prior modules)
Answer: 10

The has_any operator finds rows containing any term from a list:
ProcessEvents
| where process_commandline has_any ("schtasks", "net user", "reg add")
How many processes contains either "regedit", "spotify", or "cobaltstrike"?
I ran:
ProcessEvents
| where process_commandline has_any ("regedit", "spotify", "cobaltstrike")
Answer: 6020

